Verdict
The concessions are sound and the withdrawals are correct — Source Modes, structural modularization, and profile-as-default all fail for the reasons given. But the reply has one internal contradiction, misses two required edits without which its own accepted patches don't bite, and its §7 patch introduces a new interaction defect of exactly the class it says is now the main risk.

The contradiction: proposal-only profiles abandon the benefit
The reply holds two positions that don't coexist:

Profiles' value is "improved elicitation quality, not fewer materiality questions."
Profiles "enter only through the existing Proposal Layer (§6.4)."
The elicitation benefit lives in §3.3 — the decomposition device that offers 2–4 candidates. Routing profiles exclusively through §6.4 keeps them out of that device, so the elicitation gain doesn't materialise. And routing them into it is the §3.3 violation you just conceded.

Worse, the relocation doesn't actually clear §3.3. Presenting <proposal>Accuracy</proposal> for ratification during diagnostics is offering an invented candidate for selection, whatever container it ships in. So §3.3 needs amending either way — which is why A-Tier #6 (clarify §3.3 candidate sources) is a prerequisite for #5, not a successor to it. The ordering is backwards.

Also worth noting: §6.1 already authorises proposed rubric dimensions ("Emit proposed dimensions as <proposal> items requiring ratification"). The reply's headline profile example — proposing rubric dimensions — is existing behaviour. Profiles add nothing there.

A new defect the reply doesn't see: ratification laundering
§6.4 says proposals "carry no authority until ratified by the user." §9's NO LAUNDERING rule enumerates restating, reformatting, tabulating, upstream validation, and container crossing — but not compiler-proposed-then-ratified. So the path

compiler invents a source hierarchy → tags it <proposal> → user says "yes" → it is now user-supplied

is currently open, and §10's Provenance Mapping check only inspects §15.2 upstream labels, so it doesn't fire.

Today this is low-volume: proposals are ad hoc. Profiles industrialise it — a /profile=research that proposes a full source hierarchy converts a Tier-1 §7.1 parameter into "supplied" on one word of assent, with all the anchoring that implies. If profiles are added, §9 needs a matching clause: ratification of a compiler-originated Tier-1 value is recorded as ratified-proposal, not stated-by-user, and is disclosed in the Compile Header. Without it, profiles are a §9 bypass wearing §6.4's clothes.

Two edits the accepted patches require and the reply omits
1. Residual precedence needs the enforcement clause and a §10 amendment.

The reply's rendering drops the gate and the tier from my patch. As written it's an unenforced requirement. Worse, §10's check reads:

REVIEWER PRECEDENCE — fails if two or more reviewers are specified without a stated precedence order and without a REVIEWER_UNDEFINED gate.

Domain-scoped precedence is a stated precedence order. So the check passes while the residual gap exists. The patch is inert unless §10 is amended to "...without a stated precedence order including a residual order per §6.3...". This is completing an existing check, not adding one.

2. Any new section requires amending §16.1.

§16.1's two enumerations — pure compile-time (1, 2, 3, 5, 6.2, 10, 12–17) and mixed (4, 6.1, 6.3, 6.4, 7, 8.2, 9, 11) — jointly cover §§1–17 and the first is declared exhaustive. A new §18 Invariant Registry, a Design Principles section, and a conceptual module map are each unclassified on arrival: their shipping status is undefined, and §10's CROSS-REFERENCE check won't catch it because it only verifies that references resolve. Every one of B-Tier #8, #9 and S-Tier #4 needs §16.1 extended in the same commit.

The §7 ambiguity rule, as accepted, can produce a no-op
This is the substantive problem. "If unclear whether the task involves external factual claims, §7 applies" collides with §7's own precondition:

if no retrieval tool is declared available, the compiled prompt must prohibit external factual claims outright and gate the requirement, error_code NO_RETRIEVAL_TOOL

And §4: "Tier-1 gaps always produce blocking='true'. Only §6.1's non-adjudicative rubric absence produces blocking='false'." So NO_RETRIEVAL_TOOL is blocking.

Now run §17.6 — /fast Draft an internal training guide on our expense policy — with the new rule. Does a training guide involve external factual claims? Genuinely unclear. Ambiguity rule fires → §7 applies → no retrieval tool declared → blocking gate → the artifact halts before drafting. That is precisely the failure §17.6 exists to forbid: "The most common single invocation of this compiler must not compile to a no-op."

The fix is to scope the prohibition rather than gate the artifact:

text
- APPLICABILITY: if it is unclear whether the task involves external factual
  claims, §7 applies. Where applicability is established only by this rule and
  no retrieval tool is declared, the compiled prompt states a claim-scoped
  prohibition — no external factual claim may be made, halt if one is required —
  rather than a blocking NO_RETRIEVAL_TOOL gate. Where applicability is
  established by the task itself, the gate is emitted as normal.

That preserves the ambiguous-tiering philosophy without converting drafting tasks into halts. Note this is itself an invariant-registry entry: §7 applicability → §7 precondition → §4 blocking values → §17.6. Four sections, all individually valid, combining into a no-op. Good evidence for the reply's own thesis, and a reason to build the registry before adding the rule it describes.

On the registry's authority
One question the reply leaves open: is §18 documentation or enforcement? If documentation only, it aids maintenance but catches nothing at compile time. If enforcement, it wants a single §10 check — INVARIANT REGISTRY: fails if any registered invariant is violated — which is one check subsuming many couplings, and therefore consistent with your shared "no more validation rules" position rather than a breach of it. I'd take the enforcing version; but it's your call and it changes the patch.
